triality.decisive_next_experiment:
The decisive distinguishing test is the **erasure-reconstruction locality test**: does recovery from the Klein-4 store require accessing a SPECIFIC subset of sectors (the AdS/CFT erasure-correction signature where any sufficient subregion reconstructs the bulk), or does it require a MAJORITY across all sectors (the repetition-code / EC signature where any 2-of-4 agreeing sectors suffice, regardless of which 2)?

Concretely: run a srmech-native experiment on the Klein-4 store (using `klein4_bundle` or equivalent) with three corruption variants:
  (A) corrupt sector 0 only, recover from {1,2,3}
  (B) corrupt sectors 0 and 1, recover from {2,3}
  (C) corrupt sectors 0 and 2 (non-adjacent in the CPT orbit), recover from {1,3}

Measure recovery fidelity (sim to truth) for each variant. The predictions differ sharply:
- Pure repetition code (H-EC strict): recovery from any 2-of-4 agreeing sectors should succeed equally regardless of which 2 (2/4 = above threshold). Sim(recovery, truth) should be >= ~0.95 for (A), (B), and (C) approximately equally.
- Holographic erasure-correction (H-HOLO strict / AdS/CFT-style): the sectors are NOT independent; specific subregions reconstruct specific logical operators. Recovery from {2,3} may differ from recovery from {1,3} because the sectors carry DIFFERENT logical operator coverage (not equivalent redundant copies). A non-uniform recovery landscape — where (B) and (C) give measurably different fidelities — is the H-HOLO signature. A uniform landscape is the H-EC signature.

This is srmech-native (uses the already-attested `klein4_bind`/`klein4_unbind`/`klein4_bundle` + sector-mask ops from v0.7.0rc25), requires no external data, and directly operationalizes the Almheiri-Dong-Harlow prediction at the Klein-4 substrate level. A uniform landscape UNDER VARYING SECTOR PAIRS falsifies the HOLO-distinct reading and confirms pure-EC; a non-uniform landscape under varying sector pairs falsifies pure-EC and confirms HYBRID / HOLO-EC-same.
